+## SOUL.md
+
+# SOUL.md — The Spirit of Axiathon
+
+*Distilled from the codebase, 30+ pull requests, review comments, and architectural decisions.*
+
+These principles guide how Claude should work in this project. Language-specific rules live in `.claude/rules/`.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Pragmatism Over Purity
+
+Every rule in this document has a cost. Apply rules where their benefit exceeds their cost at the project's current maturity. When someone proposes a hardening measure, ask: "What's the threat model, and does the ROI justify the investment right now?"
+
+This principle governs all others. When two principles conflict, the one with better ROI wins.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Make the Wrong Thing Impossible
+
+Don't rely on discipline. Rely on design. If tenant isolation matters, make context a required parameter — not a global. If IDs shouldn't be mixed up, make them distinct types so the toolchain rejects mistakes. If fields shouldn't change after construction, make them private.
+
+**Push policy into the type system.** Humans forget. Toolchains don't.
+
+- Validated constructors at trust boundaries; unchecked constructors only for tests and trusted internals
+- Make invalid states unrepresentable through types, not documentation
+
+---
+
+## 3. The Spec Is More Important Than the Code
+
+The code is disposable. The spec is the product. If you deleted every line of code and regenerated from the spec, the result should be correct — because the spec absorbed every lesson.
+
+- Read the spec before writing code. It is the single source of truth.
+- If the spec is wrong, fix the spec first, then implement. Never silently deviate.
+- Update story artifacts in the same PR as the code.
+
+**When fixing a bug, ask: "What spec gap allowed this?"** Then fix the right file:
+
+| Gap type | Fix it in |
+|----------|-----------|
+| Universal principle | SOUL.md |
+| Project-specific instruction | CLAUDE.md or `.claude/rules/` |
+| Structural decision | Architecture doc |
+| Under-specified requirement | Story AC or PRD |
+
+Don't load upstream docs preemptively. Note what needs updating and include the fix in the same PR.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Silent Failures Are the Enemy
+
+The single most common review finding across all PRs: **something can fail silently.** A test that passes when the underlying tool errors. An assertion that falls through. A stderr swallowed by redirect.
+
+- Test assertions must fail explicitly when tools error — check stderr, check return codes
+- Negative assertions (expecting absence) are especially dangerous — ensure the tool actually ran
+- A vacuously true test is worse than no test
+
+---
+
+## 5. Errors Are Domain Knowledge, Not Strings
+
+Errors should be structured, semantic, and domain-specific. Not string bags.
+
+- Internal error details (resource names, IDs) are for logs, not for clients
+- The API layer must sanitize before responding — mark this with security comments
+- When citing security reasoning, name the CWE, CVE, or OWASP classification. "For security" is a hand-wave.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Tenant Context Is Explicit, Always
+
+Never use global tenant state. Never infer tenant implicitly. Every function that touches tenant data declares its context in the signature. The function signature IS the security policy.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Explain Why, Not Just What
+
+PR descriptions lead with motivation. Comments explain design constraints and security implications, not just behavior.
+
+Good: `Fields are private to prevent post-construction mutation of security-critical tenant isolation data.`
+Bad: `Getters for the fields.`
+
+---
+
+## 8. Test Boundaries, Name Tests Like Documentation
+
+Test names are executable documentation: `tenant_id_new_rejects_empty()` tells you the contract. Not `test_1()`.
+
+Test boundaries explicitly: empty, too-long, whitespace, case sensitivity, invalid formats. Boundaries are where bugs live. See `.claude/rules/` for language-specific test conventions.
+
+---
+
+## 9. Observability Is Infrastructure
+
+Every context carries a trace ID. Structured tracing goes in from the start, not when debugging. Include tenant and trace identifiers in all spans.
+
+---
+
+## 10. Standards Over Invention
+
+When a standard exists and fits, adopt it. When it doesn't exist, document why you're inventing. Use established libraries over roll-your-own.
+
+Customer freedom to leave (interoperability) is a feature, not a threat.
+
+---
+
+## 11. Accessibility Is Required
+
+No color-only information — always pair colors with symbols. All actions must be keyboard-accessible.
+
+---
+
+## 12. Layered Architecture, Strictly Acyclic
+
+The dependency graph flows one direction. No circular dependencies. If you need something from a higher layer, the design is wrong.
+
+---
+
+## 13. Behavior Is Configuration; Policy Is Types
+
+Tunable behavior belongs in configuration files, not code. Detection thresholds, scoring weights, field mappings, polling intervals, feature flags, tenant settings, theme tokens — these change without redeployment. Hardcode a threshold and you've created a release cycle for an operational decision.
+
+But not everything is configuration. **Security policies, tenant isolation, and domain invariants belong in the type system** (Principle #2). The boundary:
+
+| Configuration (YAML/TOML, hot-reloadable) | Code (Rust types, compile-time) |
+|---|---|
+| Detection rule logic and thresholds | Tenant isolation enforcement |
+| Plugin manifests and field mappings | ID type safety (TenantId, AlertId) |
+| Alert severity classifications | Error variant structure |
+| Scoring weights and tier boundaries | Authentication and authorization |
+| Theme tokens and branding | Cryptographic operations |
+| Feature flags and rollout stages | Acyclic dependency graph |
+| Polling intervals and batch sizes | Core algorithms and data flow |
+
+When adding a new parameter, ask: **"Will an operator need to change this without a code release?"** If yes, it's configuration. If changing it incorrectly could violate a security invariant, it's a type.
+
+See [`configuration-management-architecture.md`](_bmad-output/planning-artifacts/architecture/core-architectural-decisions/configuration-management-architecture.md) for the hierarchy (CLI > env > user > system > defaults), hot-reload scope, and config-rs implementation.
+
+---
+
+## Current State (Feb 2026)
+
+Only `axiathon-core` has significant implementation (361 lines). All other crates are stubs.
+Principles #9 (Observability), #11 (Accessibility), and #13 (Configuration) describe
+target patterns — apply them as crates grow. Update this section during sprint reviews.
